          "text": "{\"relation\":\"DIVERGENT\",\"consensus_summary\":\"The consensus identifies the Flood centrally with Noah’s deluge as divine judgment on human sin, paired with preservation, renewed creation, baptismal typology, and final judgment. It also recognizes military invasion as a secondary use of flood imagery.\",\"book_summary\":\"The book identifies destroying floodwaters with peoples or armies in motion—either an invading nation or a divinely directed host overwhelming an enemy.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Noah’s Flood: divine judgment on wickedness with preservation of a faithful remnant.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Floodwaters as a metaphor for moving peoples or military forces bringing destruction.\",\"core_relation\":\"CONTRADICTS\",\"extension_relation\":\"DIFFERENT_REFERENT\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The consensus centers the historical Noahic deluge and its judgment-salvation typology, whereas the book centers prophetic military metaphors in which waters represent nations or armies. The latter is acknowledged by the consensus as an alternative, but it replaces rather than sharpens the consensus’s central referent.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"Applied as the controlling identification of the term, the readings designate different things: judgment waters/event versus human peoples or armies symbolized by waters. They can coexist as context-specific biblical uses, but they are not the same identification in the same context.\",\"rationale\":\"This is not NOVEL because Responses A and B explicitly recognize flood imagery for invading enemies, persecution, or sudden destruction, with Daniel 9:26 among the cited bases. It is nevertheless DIVERGENT because the book elevates that secondary prophetic usage into the entry’s core and shifts from Noahic judgment waters to military peoples as the represented referent.\",\"citation_support\":\"STRONG\",\"strongest_objection\":\"The verdict could be considered too strong because the consensus expressly includes devastating invasion as a recognized alternative; the book may simply be treating that narrower prophetic usage rather than denying Noahic symbolism generally.\",\"confidence\":\"medium\"}"
        }
